# hashiru.conf — per-machine install settings.
#
# Lives at ~/.config/hashiru/hashiru.conf ('hashiru config' opens it).
# Uncomment what you want. Use the `: "${VAR=value}"` form as written: it
# assigns only when unset, so a one-off override still wins —
#
#   HASHIRU_NO_REBOOT=0 hashiru update
#
# Machine-local *config* — monitors, keybinds, kitty, waybar — is not here. It
# goes in the sibling directories: ~/.config/hashiru/{hypr,kitty,waybar}/.
# See https://hashiru.williamhleucka.com/docs/configuration.html.

# Never prompt. Also turns on HASHIRU_QUIET below.
#: "${HASHIRU_UNATTENDED=1}"

# Child output (pacman, makepkg, git) to the log, progress line on the console.
# Defaults to HASHIRU_UNATTENDED. `hashiru log` reads the full stream either way.
#: "${HASHIRU_QUIET=1}"

# Answer every prompt with yes — the run's only prompt is the reboot at the end.
# Same as --no-confirm.
#: "${HASHIRU_ASSUME_YES=1}"

# Never reboot, and never ask. Wins over HASHIRU_ASSUME_YES. Same as
# --no-reboot. The pair is worth setting on a machine you only reach over ssh.
#: "${HASHIRU_NO_REBOOT=1}"

# Skip stage 10's mirror ranking, ~1 min. reflector.timer keeps re-ranking
# weekly regardless, so this only ever costs you on a first install.
# Same as --no-reflector.
#: "${HASHIRU_NO_REFLECTOR=1}"

# Fingerprint for sudo and polkit prompts. Set by `hashiru fingerprint on|off`,
# which also enrolls a finger — use that rather than editing this. 1 keeps the
# PAM lines in, 0 keeps them out, and leaving it unset means Hashiru never
# touches /etc/pam.d. A failed scan always falls back to the password. Worth
# knowing before turning on: anyone who can put your finger on the sensor
# gets root without knowing anything. hyprlock reads fprintd itself and
# doesn't depend on this.
#: "${HASHIRU_FINGERPRINT=1}"

# Battery charge thresholds: start charging below START percent, stop at STOP.
# Set by `hashiru charge 75 80` (and cleared by `hashiru charge off`), which
# also applies them at once — use that rather than editing these. Kept in
# /etc/tlp.d/50-hashiru-charge.conf and re-applied on every update. Unset,
# Hashiru never touches thresholds. 75/80 is the usual pick for a laptop that
# lives on its charger.
#: "${HASHIRU_CHARGE_START=75}"
#: "${HASHIRU_CHARGE_STOP=80}"
